Ladybird counts the end edges of an inline box on the line where
its last content is, when white space ends that content. In Chrome
157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5, a span with 16px side
padding around “aaaaaaa bbbbbbbb ” that doesn’t fit its box takes two
lines in Chrome and Firefox and one in WebKit, whose line breaker leaves
the padding out as Ladybird used to. With a 24px right border, Chrome
and Firefox break; with a 24px right margin, only Chrome breaks, and
Ladybird now breaks there too. Under white-space: pre-wrap, Firefox and
WebKit break and Chrome doesn’t; Ladybird breaks.

Under white-space: break-spaces, the white space that ends a run keeps
its advance in the run’s measurement. A min-content inline-block holding
“ alpha  beta \n gamma ” in 10px Ahem is 60px by 100px in Chrome and
WebKit (70px by 60px in Firefox), and 120px by 60px in a 120px box in
all three; Ladybird now gives the same as Chrome and WebKit.
